{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgjWY8noRXWcHsaFxcTa9","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_017eCXNmPxxiip1taaQ5TPEs","name":"StructuredOutput","input":{"working":"The line opens with the clef and three sharps. The first two 32nd-note groups go G#5 A5 B5 A5 G#5 A5 F#5 G#5, then E5 F#5 G#5 F#5 E5 F#5 D#5 E5. The sharp before the D#5 is visible. These are the second half of bar 29; its first half must sit at the end of the previous line. A barline follows. The next two 32nd groups go C#5 D#5 E5 D#5 C#5 D#5 B4 C#5, then A4 B4 C#5 B4 A4 B4 G#4 A4, falling into the lower staff. Next come 16ths: F#4, then a slurred F#4 with a trill and G#4, then A4, then the slurred pairs B#4–C#5 and D#5–E5. All of this is bar 30. After the barline, the rising 16ths E#5 F#5 G#5 A5 B5 B#5 C#6 carry staccato dots. Then come a slurred C#5–B4, a dotted B4, the 16th rest, another slurred C#5–B4, and B4 C#5 C#5 at the right edge. This is all of bar 31, which ends the line. Bar 32 is not on this line. Pitches and rhythm match throughout. The only difference is beaming: the copyist beams the 32nds in groups of eight.","right":true,"insights":"The line was fairly easy to match. The distinctive 32nd-note runs anchor the start, and the rising run with staccato dots up to C#6, followed by the rest-and-slur figure, marks bar 31. The heavy beams and the faded, speckled image make single noteheads hard to read. A clear barline at the right edge would have confirmed that the line ends exactly with bar 31, not partway into bar 32."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":6202,"cache_read_input_tokens":523,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":6202},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagno